{
  "image_name": "b031f9e862448d37b380319472a88cd4.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure explains how an electrochromic smart window is built and how it can switch between different optical states. At the top, the device is shown as a stack of layers between two transparent conductive electrodes: an electrochromic layer, an electrolyte, and an ion-storage layer. The window is then placed in a frame and illustrated as controlling incoming sunlight, with separate paths for visible light and infrared radiation.\n\nThe lower part compares three operating modes. In the bright state, the electrochromic layer appears largely transparent, so both visible light and infrared pass through strongly. In the cool state, the layer contains added electrons and ions associated with nanocrystals, and the window seems to selectively reduce infrared transmission while still allowing much of the visible light through. In the dark state, further charging leads to stronger optical blocking, so both infrared and visible light are suppressed more strongly.\n\nOverall, the main message is that the multilayer smart window can dynamically tune solar transmission. By moving ions and electrons into the active layer, it shifts from clear, to heat-blocking, to darkened, enabling separate control of daylight and solar heat.",
